<abraham_de_moivre_studied_work_of_isaac_newton> <base.kwebbase.kwconnection.sentence> "On his release in 1686, he fled to England, where he made a modest living tutoring in mathematics, and continued to study recent mathematical texts, the most notable being Newton's 'Principia Mathematica.' In 1692 he met the astronomer Halley who was so impressed by him that he read his first paper (on Newton's calculus), to the Royal Society in 1695." .
